    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 8. Content standards: B8.2.3.1.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| state the meaning of the four inequality symbols |  |  |\n| translate a word problem into an inequality |  |  |\n| translate an inequality into words |  |  |\n| explain why an inequality has many solutions |  |  |\n| solve a linear inequality using the idea of balance |  |  |\n| reverse the symbol when multiplying or dividing by a negative number |  |  |\n| show the solution of an inequality on a number line |  |  |\n| solve a real-life problem using an inequality |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory. Do not look back at the Learner's Book yet.*\n\n**1.** Solve 3x + 7 = 25\n\n**2.** Solve 5x − 4 = 2x + 11\n\n**3.** Which is greater, −3 or −7?\n\n**4.** Find −12 ÷ −3\n\n**5.** Expand 2(x − 4)\n\n**6.** Write down three whole numbers greater than 5\n\n**7.** What does the symbol > mean?\n\n**8.** Solve x/4 = 3\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** The symbol ≥ means is greater than or … to\n\n**2.** The symbol ≤ means is … than or equal to\n\n**3.** A statement using >, <, ≥ or ≤ is called an\n\n**4.** On a number line, a solution that includes the end value is shown with a … circle\n\n**5.** A solution that excludes the end value is shown with an … circle\n\n**6.** The set of all values that satisfy an inequality is called its … set\n\n**7.** Explain in your own words why x > 5 has many solutions while x = 5 has only one.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** When both sides of an inequality are divided by a negative number, the symbol must be\n\n> **Guided practice — Solving 5x − 3 ≤ 12**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nTreat it exactly like an equation while the numbers stay positive.\n\nAdd 3 to both sides: 5x ≤ 12 + 3 = ____\n\nDivide both sides by 5: x ≤ 15 ÷ 5 = ____\n\n**The solution set** is every number less than or equal to 3.\n\n**Check with two values.** x = 3 gives 12 ≤ 12 ✓. x = 4 gives 17 ≤ 12 ✗, as expected.\n\n**On a number line**, put a filled circle at 3 and an arrow pointing left.\n\n> **Guided practice — Solving −3x > 9**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nDivide both sides by −3. Because the divisor is negative, the symbol must turn round.\n\n9 ÷ (−3) = ____\n\nSo the solution is x ______ −3 (write the correct symbol)\n\n**Why the reversal.** 2 < 5 is true. Multiply both sides by −1: −2 and −5. Now −2 is **greater** than −5, so the symbol has flipped.\n\n**Check.** x = −4 gives −3(−4) = 12 > 9 ✓, and x = −2 gives 6 > 9 ✗. So x < −3 is right.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Write in symbols: (a) x is greater than 7 (b) y is at most 12 (c) n is at least 5\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Write in words: (a) x ≤ −2 (b) 3 < y ≤ 8\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Solve 5x − 3 ≤ 12.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Solve 3x + 4 > 19.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Solve 2(x − 4) ≥ 6.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** Solve −3x > 9.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**7.** Solve 7 − 2x ≤ 1.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** Solve 4x − 5 < 2x + 7.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**9.** List the whole-number solutions of 2 < x ≤ 6\n\n**10.** Show the solution of x ≥ −1 on a number line.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** A learner solves −2x > 8 and writes x > −4. Explain the error and give the correct solution.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**2.** Solve 3 ≤ 2x − 1 < 11 and list the whole-number solutions.\n\n**3.** Explain why x > 3 and x ≥ 3 do not have the same solution set, and give one number that belongs to only one of them.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** The perimeter of a square must be less than 48 cm. Write an inequality for its side s and solve it.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**5.** Twice a number, decreased by 5, is at least 17. Form an inequality and find the smallest whole number that satisfies it.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Applied task**\n\n*Mathematics on a real situation. Show all your working.*\n\n**1.** A tro-tro may carry at most 18 passengers. There are already 7 on board. (a) Write an inequality for the number n who may still board. (b) Solve it. (c) State the largest number that may board and explain why n cannot be negative.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**2.** A learner has GH₵50 and wants to buy pens costing GH₵6 each, keeping at least GH₵8 for transport. (a) Write an inequality for the number p of pens. (b) Solve it. (c) State how many pens she can buy and how much she has left.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**3.** A hall is hired for GH₵400 plus GH₵15 for each guest. The budget is GH₵1,300. (a) Write an inequality for the number g of guests. (b) Solve it. (c) State the largest number of guests. (d) If the hire charge rises to GH₵500, find the new limit.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Write in symbols: (a) x is at least 5 (b) y is less than −2. **[2]**\n\n**2.** Solve 5x − 3 ≤ 12. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Solve 2(x − 4) ≥ 6. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Solve −3x > 9 and explain why the symbol changes. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** List the whole-number solutions of 2 < x ≤ 6 and show the solution set on a number line. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**6.** A learner has GH₵50 and buys pens at GH₵6 each, keeping at least GH₵8 for transport. Form an inequality and solve it to find how many pens she can buy. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Applied task | 3 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n### Review 2 — Algebra\n\n*Chapters 6 to 8. Time: 40 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n> **Review 2 — 30 marks**\n\n*Work without help and without the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** Find the gradient of the line through (2, 3) and (6, 11). **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**2.** Write down the gradient and the y-intercept of y = 7 − 2x. **[2]**\n\n**3.** Complete a table of values for y = 3x − 2 for x = −2 to 2. **[3]**\n\n| **x** | **−2** | **−1** | **0** | **1** | **2** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| y |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n**4.** On the grid, draw the graph of y = 2x − 1 for x from −1 to 3. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 14 lines]_\n\n**5.** Expand and simplify 3(2x − 5) − 2(x − 4). **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**6.** Expand (2x − 1)(3x + 4). **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**7.** Factorise (a) 8x² + 12x (b) x² − 81. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** Factorise x² + 7x + 12. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**9.** Solve 2(x − 4) ≥ 6. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**10.** Solve −3x > 9 and explain why the symbol changes. **[2]**\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Review 2 | 30 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Geometry and Measurement · Sub-Strand: Shapes and Space*"
