    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 9. Content standards: B8.3.1.1, B8.3.1.2.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| name the angles formed when a transversal crosses two parallel lines |  |  |\n| use alternate, corresponding and co-interior angle facts, giving reasons |  |  |\n| calculate unknown angles in a diagram and justify each step |  |  |\n| use the fact that the angles of a triangle add to 180° |  |  |\n| use the exterior angle property of a triangle |  |  |\n| find the sum of the interior angles of a polygon |  |  |\n| find each interior and exterior angle of a regular polygon |  |  |\n| construct triangles and bisect angles with ruler and compasses |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory. Do not look back at the Learner's Book yet.*\n\n**1.** Find the complement of 35°\n\n**2.** Find the supplement of 118°\n\n**3.** Two straight lines cross; one angle is 65°. Give the angle vertically opposite it\n\n**4.** How many degrees are there in a triangle?\n\n**5.** Find 180 − (47 + 68)\n\n**6.** How many sides has a hexagon?\n\n**7.** Find 360 ÷ 8\n\n**8.** What instrument draws a circle?\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A line that cuts across two or more other lines is called a\n\n**2.** Equal angles lying on the same side of the transversal, one above and one below the parallel lines, are called … angles\n\n**3.** Equal angles lying on opposite sides of the transversal, between the parallel lines, are called … angles......\n\n**4.** Angles between the parallel lines on the same side of the transversal add to 180° and are called … angles.\n\n**5.** The angle formed when a side of a triangle is produced is called an … angle\n\n**6.** A polygon with all sides and all angles equal is called a … polygon\n\n**7.** Write the formula for the sum of the interior angles of a polygon of n sides.\n\n**8.** The exterior angles of any polygon always add up to\n\n> **Guided practice — Finding angles between parallel lines, with reasons**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nTwo parallel lines are crossed by a transversal. One angle is 72°.\n\nThe angle corresponding to it is also 72°, because corresponding angles are ______\n\nThe angle alternate to it is also ______\n\nThe co-interior angle is 180° − 72° = ______\n\n**Always give the reason.** In an examination, *72° (corresponding angles)* earns both marks; *72°* alone earns one.\n\n> **Guided practice — Finding the interior angles of a regular octagon**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nUse the formula: sum of interior angles = (n − 2) × 180°.\n\nFor an octagon, n = 8, so the sum is (8 − 2) × 180 = ________\n\nThe polygon is regular, so divide by 8: each interior angle is ________\n\n**By the exterior angle instead.** Each exterior angle is 360 ÷ 8 = ______°, and the interior angle is 180 − 45 = ______°. The two methods agree.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n\n\n**1.** Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal and one angle is 72°. Write down (a) the corresponding angle (b) the alternate angle (c) the co-interior angle, giving a reason for each.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**2.** Find the third angle of a triangle whose other angles are 47° and 68°\n\n**3.** A triangle has angles 3x, 4x and 5x. Find x and all three angles.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**4.** An exterior angle of a triangle is 110° and one opposite interior angle is 45°. Find the other opposite interior angle.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**5.** Find the sum of the interior angles of (a) a pentagon (b) an octagon (c) a twelve-sided polygon.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** Find each interior angle of (a) a regular hexagon (b) a regular octagon.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**7.** Find each exterior angle of a regular decagon (10 sides)\n\n**8.** A regular polygon has an exterior angle of 24°. Find the number of sides.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**9.** Construct a triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 10 cm, and measure its largest angle.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**10.** Construct an angle of 60° and bisect it, leaving all construction arcs showing.\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** In a diagram of two parallel lines, one angle is 3x and the co-interior angle is 2x. Find x and both angles.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**2.** The angles of a quadrilateral are x, 2x, 3x and 4x. Find x and the largest angle.\n\n**3.** Explain why the exterior angles of every polygon add to 360°, whatever the number of sides.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**4.** A regular polygon has an interior angle of 156°. Find the number of sides.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**5.** Construct a triangle with a base of 7 cm, a base angle of 60° and another base angle of 45°. Measure the third angle and the two other sides.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Applied task**\n\n*Mathematics on a real situation. Show all your working.*\n\n**1.** A roof truss is a triangle with a base angle of 35° at each end. (a) Find the angle at the apex. (b) A brace is added from the apex to the midpoint of the base, making two triangles. State the three angles of one of them. (c) Explain why the brace meets the base at 90°.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**2.** A tiled floor is made only of regular polygons meeting at a point with no gaps. (a) Find the interior angle of a regular hexagon. (b) Show that three hexagons fit exactly round a point. (c) Show that regular pentagons cannot tile a floor in this way.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**3.** A surveyor marks out a plot as a triangle with sides 6 m, 8 m and 10 m. (a) Construct it to a scale of 1 cm to 1 m. (b) Measure the largest angle. (c) State what kind of triangle it is and why. (d) Find its area.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal and one angle is 72°. Write down the corresponding, alternate and co- interior angles, with a reason for each. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**2.** A triangle has angles 3x, 4x and 5x. Find x and the three angles. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**3.** Find the sum of the interior angles of an octagon and the size of each interior angle if it is regular. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** A regular polygon has an exterior angle of 24°. Find the number of sides. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Construct a triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 10 cm, leaving your arcs showing, and measure its largest angle. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**6.** An exterior angle of a triangle is 110° and one opposite interior angle is 45°. Find the other opposite interior angle and explain the property you used. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Applied task | 3 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Geometry and Measurement · Sub-Strand: Measurement*"
