    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 10. Content standards: B8.3.2.1, B8.3.2.2.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| calculate the area of a circle, a semicircle and a ring |  |  |\n| state Pythagoras' theorem |  |  |\n| use Pythagoras' theorem to find the hypotenuse |  |  |\n| use Pythagoras' theorem to find a shorter side |  |  |\n| test whether a triangle is right-angled |  |  |\n| solve real problems using Pythagoras' theorem |  |  |\n| write a vector as a column and find its magnitude |  |  |\n| use a given trigonometric ratio to find a missing side; add two vectors and find the resultant |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory. Do not look back at the Learner's Book yet.*\n\n**1.** Find 12²\n\n**2.** Find √169\n\n**3.** Taking π = 22/7, find the circumference of a circle of diameter 14 cm\n\n**4.** Find the area of a rectangle 12 cm by 8 cm\n\n**5.** Find 5² + 12²\n\n**6.** Find √(25² − 7²)\n\n**7.** Find the back bearing of 065°\n\n**8.** Is 3, 4, 5 a right-angled triple?\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n**1.** The longest side of a right-angled triangle is called the\n\n**2.** State Pythagoras' theorem in words.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** The region between two circles with the same centre is called a\n\n**4.** Half a circle is called a\n\n**5.** A quantity with both size and direction is called a\n\n**6.** The size of a vector is called its\n\n**7.** Explain in your own words how you know which side of a right-angled triangle is the hypotenuse.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** The single vector that replaces two vectors added together is called the\n\n> **Guided practice — Finding the hypotenuse of a triangle with sides 9 cm and 12 cm**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nWrite the theorem: c² = a² + b².\n\n9² = ____\n\n12² = ____\n\n81 + 144 = ______\n\nc = √225 = ____ **Check.** The hypotenuse must be longer than either side, and 15 > 12. ✓\n\n> **Guided practice — Finding the area of a ring with outer radius 14 cm and inner radius 7 cm**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\nArea of the large circle = πR² = 22/7 × 14 × 14 = ________\n\nArea of the small circle = πr² = 22/7 × 7 × 7 = ________\n\nArea of the ring = 616 − 154 = ________\n\n**Short cut.** A = π(R² − r²) = 22/7 × (196 − 49) = 22/7 × 147 = 462 cm². Same answer, fewer steps.\n\n**Units.** Area is always in square units.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Taking π = 22/7, find the area of a circle of radius 21 cm.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Taking π = 22/7, find the area of a semicircle of radius 14 cm.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Taking π = 22/7, find the area of a ring with outer radius 14 cm and inner radius 7 cm.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Find the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with sides (a) 5 cm and 12 cm (b) 8 cm and 15 cm.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Find the third side of a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is 41 cm and one side 40 cm.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** A right-angled triangle has sides 9 cm and 12 cm about the right angle. Find the hypotenuse.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**7.** Show whether a triangle with sides 7 cm, 24 cm and 25 cm is right-angled.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** Show whether a triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 11 cm is right-angled.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**9.** A vector is 3 units east and 4 units north. Find its magnitude.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n10. (a) Find the magnitude of the vector 5 units east and 12 units south. (b) In a right-angled triangle, tan θ = 3/4 and the adjacent side is 12 cm. Find the opposite side and the hypotenuse.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** A ladder 13 m long rests against a wall with its foot 5 m from the wall. (a) How far up the wall does it reach? (b) The foot is pulled out to 12 m from the wall. How far up does it reach now? (c) Comment on the safety of the second position.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**2.** A rectangular field is 40 m by 30 m. Find the length of the diagonal path across it, and how much shorter that path is than walking round two sides.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n**3.** A circular garden of radius 21 m has a path 3.5 m wide round the outside. Taking π = 22/7, find the area of the path.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**4.** A boat sails 8 km east then 6 km north. (a) Find its distance from the start. (b) Find the magnitude of the resultant if it then sails 8 km west. (c) Explain the difference between the distance travelled and the resultant.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Show that a triangle with sides 9 cm, 40 cm and 41 cm is right-angled, and find its area.\n\n> **Applied task**\n\n*Mathematics on a real situation. Show all your working.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A circular tank of radius 7 m stands in the middle of a square yard of side 20 m. Taking π = 22/7, (a) find the area of the yard, (b) find the area of the tank base, (c) find the area of yard left uncovered, (d) express the uncovered area as a percentage of the yard, to the nearest whole number.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**2.** A gate is a rectangle 2.4 m wide and 1.8 m high, strengthened by a diagonal brace. (a) Find the length of the brace. (b) The brace costs GH₵85 a metre. Find its cost. (c) A second brace is added the other way. Find the total cost of both.\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n**3.** A surveyor walks 9 m north, then 12 m east, then 9 m south. (a) Draw the journey to a scale of 1 cm to 3 m. (b) Find the total distance walked. (c) Find how far she is from her starting point. (d) Explain why Pythagoras' theorem is not needed for part (c).\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n\n\n**1.** Taking π = 22/7, find the area of a circle of radius 21 cm. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**2.** Taking π = 22/7, find the area of a ring with outer radius 14 cm and inner radius 7 cm. **[3]**\n\n3. (a) Find the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with sides 5 cm and 12 cm. (b) If sin θ = 5/13 and the hypotenuse is 26 cm, find the opposite side. [3]\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** A ladder 13 m long has its foot 5 m from a wall. Find how far up the wall it reaches. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Show whether a triangle with sides 7 cm, 24 cm and 25 cm is right-angled. **[4]**\n\n**6.** A rectangular field is 40 m by 30 m. Find the length of the diagonal and state how much shorter it is than walking round two sides. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — box]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**Section**\n\n**Out of**\n\n**My score**\n\n**Date**\n\n\n\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Applied task | 3 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Geometry and Measurement · Sub-Strand: Position and Transformation*"
